<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Another source dealing with using Lemke to solve for Nash equilibrium suggested that you could just randomly select a pivot.</div></blockquote>Well one could store a counter for the system, always initialized to a specific value (like zero).  Then every time you need to choose between pivots, increment the counter and take the modulus of the number of choices.  For a given system starting at a given initial state, this keeps the determinism for debugging purposes.<br><br>EDIT: this is the same as seeding the random number generator with the same seed each time (and making sure nobody else is sharing the generator)<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=1626">bone</a> — Mon Jan 03, 2011 3:21 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
